<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seven Sealands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seven Frisian Sealands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seven Sealands were the self-governing coastal lands of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee">Zuiderzee</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, which formed a loose league during the Frisian Freedom. Their delegates met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upstalsboom">Upstalsboom</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a> to settle disputes and agree <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common laws</a>, the best known being the Upstalsboom statutes of 1323. The number seven was more traditional than exact.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seven-sealands.jpg" alt="Seven Sealands" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Swarte Kees · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yndieling_fan_de_S%C3%A2n_Fryske_Seelannen_neffens_it_%27traktaat%27_fan_1417_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
